  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe are to find a step function h(x) on [0,2] that minimizes the maximum overlap integral, specifically the Erd\u0151s minimum overlappartition problem constant C. The given initial solution has n=42 samples and we aim to lower the score from 0.3808 to a lower value. The limitation of the approach is optimizing combined with multi-resolution analysis, leveraging both genetic and local search. This approach aims to:\n- Scrutinize the initial solution to detect potentially improvable patterns\n- Conduct a controlled search in the hyperparameters space to seek a lower bound\n- Maintain parameter constraints (h(x) in [0,1] and integral of 1)\n- Track improvements and converge on a near-optimal solution within time limits\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Notation: h as a vector of n-point samples over [0,2], each h[i] in [0,1], sum(h) = n/2\n- Cost: C\u2085 = max_{k} (1/n) * (numpy.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')[k] )\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085, where C\u2085 is a real number and lowering it is the goal\n- We use discrete representations with dx = 2.0/n in integral computation\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nClient provides:\n- initial_h_values: The seed solution (n=42)\n\n Function flow:\n a. Setup dx and parent-specific variables\n b. function compute_c5(h): calculates the cost using scipy's correlate\n c. function is_feasible(h): ensures values in [0,1] and integer constraint\n d. function position_energy(pos_index, h): mutation\n e. function select_mutation_operator(): strategy selecting\n f. Extended Kalman Filter (EKF)-inspired hybrid local search\n g. Hierarchical optimization:\n    - Initialization using random feasible neighbors\n    - Primary local/search with EKF and multidimensional scaling (MDS)\n    - Global search via league championship algorithm (lca)\n    - Multi-resolution exploitation (small n improvement)\n h. Progress tracking within budget_s (1100 seconds)\n\n4. Search choices:\n- Primary search: straddling between global and local search using LCA and gradient descent\n- Secondary: controlled gradient evolution with multiresolution ($n changes) search\n- Budget: Use global search for n\u00b110% then local search\n- Limit to recommended n=40 - 50 for good balance\n\n5. Numerical and computational robustness:\n- Problems: Overflow from correlation, converge on local minima\n- Solutions: Clamping, adaptive gradient scaling, early termination\n- Progress recorded: Best/Initial solution score, time spent\n\n6. Runtim Safety and contract:\n- Time budget checked every 100 iterations\n- Progressive n increase on initial score observation\n- Progress every time: Print best new C\u2085\n- On failure: returns initial solution",
